{"name":"Rye Bread","hex":"#cdbea1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rye bread","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bread made partly or entirely with rye flour, which may be wholemeal (wholegrain) flour or not."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rye_bread"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rye bread","blocks":[{"text":"Rye bread is a type of bread made with various proportions of flour from rye grain. It can be light or dark in color, depending on the type of flour used and the addition of coloring agents, and is typically denser than bread made from wheat flour. Compared to white bread, it is higher in fiber, darker in color, and stronger in flavor. The world's largest exporter of rye bread is Poland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Market_Bread%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia.jpg/330px-Market_Bread%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rye_bread","wikidata":"Q3893120"},{"title":"Rye","blocks":[{"text":"Rye is a grass grown extensively as a grain, a cover crop and a forage crop. It is grown principally in an area from Eastern and Northern Europe into Russia. It is much more tolerant of cold weather and poor soil than other cereals, making it useful in those regions; its vigorous growth suppresses weeds and provides abundant forage for animals early in the year. It is a member of the wheat tribe (Triticeae) which includes the cereals wheat and barley. It is likely that rye arrived in Europe as a secondary crop, meaning that it was a minor admixture in wheat as a result of Vavilovian mimicry, and was only later cultivated in its own right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ear_of_rye.jpg/330px-Ear_of_rye.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rye","wikidata":"Q12099"},{"title":"Bread","blocks":[{"text":"Bread is a baked food product made from water, flour, and often yeast. It is a staple food across the world, particularly in Europe and the Middle East. Throughout recorded history and around the world, it has been an important part of many cultures' diets. It is one of the oldest human-made foods, having been of significance since the dawn of agriculture, and plays an essential role in both religious rituals and secular culture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Korb_mit_Br%C3%B6tchen.JPG/330px-Korb_mit_Br%C3%B6tchen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bread","wikidata":"Q7802"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Pumpernickel.jpg/500px-Pumpernickel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Pumpernickel.jpg/1280px-Pumpernickel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Pumpernickel.jpg/500px-Pumpernickel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A very dense wholegrain pumpernickel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APumpernickel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg/500px-Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg/1280px-Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg/500px-Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t